-- title:
--   Lefebvre's variations, No. 223 — on a theme by Burau: the Burau representation of B_4 is injective on the subgroup generated by the first two Artin generators
-- statement:
--   Let $\rho_4\colon B_4\to GL_4(\mathbb{Z}[t,t^{-1}])$ be the unreduced Burau representation and $H=\langle\sigma_1,\sigma_2\rangle\le B_4$ the subgroup generated by the first two Artin generators (a copy of $B_3$). Then $\rho_4$ is injective on $H$:
--   $$x,y\in H,\ \rho_4(x)=\rho_4(y)\ \Longrightarrow\ x=y .$$
--
--   On $H$ the matrix $\rho_4(\beta)$ is block diagonal $\rho_3(\beta)\oplus 1$, and the Burau representation $\rho_3$ of $B_3$ is faithful (Magnus and Peluso), which gives the claim. Faithfulness on all of $B_4$ is a well-known open problem.
--
--   **Formalization Note** `BraidsLinksMCG.sigma (n := 4) ⟨i, _⟩` is the Artin generator `σ_{i+1}` of `ArtinBraidGroup 4` (0-based index in `Fin (4 - 1)`); the subgroup is `Subgroup.closure {σ₁, σ₂}` coerced to a set, and `Set.InjOn (BurauFaithful.burauRep 4)` is injectivity of the platform's unreduced Burau representation on that set. No injectivity of the inclusion `B₃ → B₄` is needed.
--
--   **Why this is a variation.** Partial result towards the open problem BurauFaithful.burau_faithful_four: injectivity of the Burau representation of B_4 restricted to the copy of B_3 generated by sigma_1 and sigma_2, where the matrices are rho_3 plus a trivial block. It follows formally from the parent and, mathematically, from the faithfulness of rho_3 (Magnus and Peluso).
--
--   **Attribution.** This statement belongs to *Lefebvre's variations*, a series proposed by Erwan Lefebvre: each variation plays an open problem of the platform at its first non-trivial size, so that it can be settled and then reused to attack the open problem itself.
-- source:
--   Parent: BurauFaithful.burau_faithful_four (open on the platform: injectivity of burauRep 4 on all of B_4). This is the restriction of that injectivity to the subgroup generated by the first two generators, so it follows formally from the parent.
--   Source: W. Magnus and A. Peluso, 'On a theorem of V. I. Arnol'd', Comm. Pure Appl. Math. 22 (1969) (faithfulness of rho_3, cited from memory); the platform also has BurauFaithful.burau_faithful_three (Proved).

import Definitions.Def_BurauFaithful_UnreducedBurau

set_option autoImplicit false

namespace LefebvreVariations

/-- The unreduced Burau representation of `B₄` is injective on the subgroup generated by `σ₁` and
`σ₂`, a copy of `B₃`: on it `burauRep 4` is `ρ₃ ⊕ 1`, and `ρ₃` is faithful (Magnus–Peluso). A partial
result towards `BurauFaithful.burau_faithful_four`. -/
theorem burau_four_injective_on_braid_three :
    Set.InjOn (BurauFaithful.burauRep 4)
      (Subgroup.closure
        {BraidsLinksMCG.sigma (n := 4) ⟨0, by norm_num⟩,
         BraidsLinksMCG.sigma (n := 4) ⟨1, by norm_num⟩} :
        Set (BraidsLinksMCG.ArtinBraidGroup 4)) := by
  sorry

end LefebvreVariations
